WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.340
You hear it everywhere. DLSS this, DLSS that. But&nbsp;
what is it? And why is it doing the impossible?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:15.180
This AI-based technology is being talked about&nbsp;
the media a great deal, and it is supposedly a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:22.860
way of dramatically speeding up computer games&nbsp;
and virtual worlds. Does this really work?

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:30.000
Well, when Jensen Huang, NVIDIA’s CEO says that&nbsp;
every single pixel on your screen for these games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.040
will not be rendered, but will be generated soon,&nbsp;
presumably by an AI, that really got my attention.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:45.900
That sounds insane, and if hearing this you think&nbsp;
that this cannot possibly be true, I don’t blame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:52.260
you for a second. We can’t just create all this&nbsp;
footage, there has to be a proper system that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:59.940
can compute reflections, diffuse interactions,&nbsp;
and all that. Right? Well, not quite. In 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:07.020
I had a little project where I wanted to generate&nbsp;
photorealistic images of material models quickly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:13.260
Now, generating an image through ray&nbsp;
tracing took up to 40 to 60 seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:20.280
and that was a bit too long for my taste.&nbsp;
So I tried to write a neural renderer that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:26.580
could take just the description of a material&nbsp;
model, and it would immediately, in real time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:34.620
synthesize what it would look like. And it was&nbsp;
able to do all this not in 60 seconds, but in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:45.060
3 milliseconds. Yes, it was 20,000 times faster.&nbsp;
However, it was limited to this very scene. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:51.600
how did this research field improve in the last&nbsp;
5 years? Do we have anything more usable now?

00:01:51.600 --> 00:02:00.000
Oh boy, if I could tell you. Well, meet DLSS. Deep&nbsp;
Learning Super Sampling. This is a system where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.520
hardware and software works together to&nbsp;
create an incredible experience where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:12.780
games and virtual worlds really run faster&nbsp;
than what should be possible. So, how?

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:21.720
Well, as of DLSS version 3, get this, by&nbsp;
generating 7 of every 8 pixel that appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:30.240
on the screen. Yes, more than 85% of the pixels&nbsp;
are just generated, not computed by for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:38.760
ray tracing or traditional techniques. That&nbsp;
sounds flat out impossible. But, it is possible.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:45.660
The first step is running super resolution in&nbsp;
real time. This means that a coarse image goes in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:51.300
and an AI finds out if we pretended&nbsp;
that this is a fine image instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:56.040
what are the details that are missing,&nbsp;
and it synthesizes those details.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:03.600
Second, optical flow. We can look at two adjacent&nbsp;
frames of the video game, and try to estimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:10.560
what has happened between them, and where things&nbsp;
are going. With this, entire intermediate frames&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:16.380
can be synthesized, creating the illusion as if&nbsp;
the game was running smoother than our hardware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:22.440
can run it. Combining the two together, with&nbsp;
a pool of very few pixels that are computed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:29.820
both image quality and smoothness can be improved&nbsp;
at the same time. What a time to be alive!

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:37.440
But it does not stop there. It gets better!&nbsp;
They have also announced DLSS 3.5, hopefully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.840
all this can be improved even further&nbsp;
through something that they call ray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:49.560
reconstruction. Oh my goodness, this&nbsp;
is going to be so good. But, how?

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:56.340
Well, normally, when we perform ray tracing, we&nbsp;
simulate the path of millions and millions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:02.040
rays, and bounce them around in a scene.&nbsp;
And if we can’t afford to wait for up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:08.280
hours at a time for an image, we will have to&nbsp;
settle for a really noisy image. Like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:14.700
Denoising techniques exist that try to guess&nbsp;
what the missing information could be, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:21.960
are not perfect. Not even close. Some important&nbsp;
details between objects can be missed entirely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:29.340
and still, reflections can be a lot less clear&nbsp;
than in a full simulation. And unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:35.820
we have a problem. It gets even worse.&nbsp;
This image will undergo an upscaling step,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:42.420
where these errors get magnified even more.&nbsp;
Denoising and upscaling are two separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:50.280
steps typically done by two separate models, so&nbsp;
why not do both of them with the same AI model?

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:57.180
And now, ray reconstruction enters the scene.&nbsp;
It learned on a ton more information than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:02.640
previous models, 5 times more training&nbsp;
data was given to it. And look at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:08.760
step. This one is right on the money. This is&nbsp;
tailored to retain high-frequency information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:15.300
fine details to make sure that before the&nbsp;
upscaling step, we have the highest quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:22.380
information available. So, is it better than&nbsp;
what denoisers did before? Oh my goodness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:28.980
look at that! We finally get that shadowy&nbsp;
region back that was filled in by incorrect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:35.040
information with the denoisers before.&nbsp;
Yummy! So good! And that high-frequency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:39.960
information retaining capability can&nbsp;
also be witnessed here. Loving it.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:46.560
Now note that ideally, here, we talk about&nbsp;
peer-reviewed research papers where we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:52.980
see all the weak points and failure cases, that&nbsp;
is my home turf. This is not a research paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:58.800
so it is harder for me to find the flaws,&nbsp;
so please bear in mind that it may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:04.380
weaknesses that were not shown here. And in&nbsp;
the meantime, this technology is being handed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:10.740
out to millions and millions of people all&nbsp;
around the world, and it is incredible. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:15.840
this will be available only for the&nbsp;
shiniest, newest graphics card owners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:21.720
right? Well, hold on to your papers Fellow&nbsp;
Scholars, because here comes the best part:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:29.160
no! This comes to all RTX graphics cards, even&nbsp;
the older ones that you can pick up for a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:36.240
hundred bucks. This can breathe new life to aging&nbsp;
hardware which is highly appreciated. Note that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:43.020
the particular game or app you’re running has to&nbsp;
be ready for DLSS3.5 for all this magic to happen.

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:48.540
Now, I am a light transport researcher&nbsp;
by trade, I dream with rays of light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:56.040
and ray tracing if you will, so I am incredibly&nbsp;
happy to see this technology make it to the hands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:01.860
of real users in the real world in such&nbsp;
an incredible way. And make no mistake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:07.860
this is not perfect by any means, there are&nbsp;
games where it does not appear to work very well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:14.460
and some people prefer to not use it at&nbsp;
all, anywhere. However, note that this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:20.880
still nascent technology, and it can already&nbsp;
synthesize more than 85% of these frames,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:26.760
which is something that I thought would only&nbsp;
be possible in a science fiction movie. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:34.380
not even there. I am absolutely stunned by these&nbsp;
results, even with their weaknesses. And don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:40.440
forget to invoke the First Law of Papers. The&nbsp;
First Law Of Papers says that research is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:45.900
process. Do not look at where we are, look at&nbsp;
where we will be two more papers down the line.

